From 2b3c0555e2d646d29ae3d351e2ad164df91fbc08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Trotter <33046718+sjtrotter@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 05:59:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] RDP: handle PKCS11 client certificates in WebKit

Answer WebKitGTK client-certificate and certificate-PIN challenges raised
by the AAD authentication WebView with certificates discovered through
PKCS #11. Discovery runs p11tool asynchronously with a bounded timeout,
bounded output, and a cancellable, so a stalled reader cannot block the
GTK main thread indefinitely.

Challenges are only answered when they come from the verified HTTPS
authentication origin on the effective port 443 and are not proxy
challenges. Entra ID commonly issues the client-certificate challenge from
the dedicated "certauth." subdomain of the login authority (for example
certauth.login.microsoftonline.com), so the trusted host is either the
exact verified authority or exactly "certauth." followed by that
authority. The relationship is computed once as an expected name and
compared exactly; no suffix or substring matching is performed, so
lookalike names such as certauth.login.microsoftonline.com.example or
xcertauth.login.microsoftonline.com are rejected. PIN challenges must
name the same host that received the certificate.

The dialogs run nested main loops, during which the WebView toplevel can
be destroyed by a policy decision or a window-manager close. The handler
therefore holds a strong reference on the toplevel for its whole
lifetime, records destruction through the "destroy" signal, and re-checks
that the window is still alive before reusing it as a transient parent or
continuing with a challenge. A challenge whose window disappeared is
cancelled exactly once.

Generated with AI assistance (Claude); reviewed and tested by the author.
